Marvel Ultimate Alliance 2's scene profile for the scene upscaler of 0161 to 0167. MUA2 draws its 3D scene into one
surface that fits the EDRAM at 2x MSAA: color base 0 (k_8_8_8_8) and depth base 1034 (D24FS8, depth 1 near), pitch
1040, 1040x624 guest pixels; its 1280x720 composite is a pitch 880 surface that also starts at color base 0. The mua2
profile pins that surface (PinnedSceneSurface): only draws and resolves at its pitch are the scene's; the scene's own
size, not the video mode's, sizes the upscaler's output (1040x624 times the resolution scale), which the game's
composite then brings to the framebuffer; and the pass's last resolve must be the whole surface, which gets the output,
while the full-scene copy before it, which a 3D draw of the scene reads, keeps the scene's input stretched. Its sky is
drawn after the scene's depth resolve, into the depth range below the scene's, past the scene's infinite far plane, so
depth is taken at each resolve, the scene's viewport is the one with the widest depth range, and scene_motion clamps
depths to a range that takes the sky's as infinitely far (unbounded for other profiles, which leaves their motion
vectors as they were; the bytecode is fxc's, through d3dcompiler_47's D3DCompile with /O3's flag). Targets drawn before
the scene, such as shadow maps, stay at the full scale, and the upscaler turns off when the scene's draws hold no camera
projection for 600 frames. mua1 and mua1-gold leave all of it off, so both games are unchanged.

